<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      The location family declared here (SPEC.md §3.4; declared with the
      maintainer's approval, 2026-08-22): the departure test needs a fine fix —
      §6.6's accuracy gate cannot pass on coarse — and COARSE is declared
      beside it because Android 12+ lets the user downgrade the grant, so a
      request for FINE alone would be refused outright instead of downgraded.
      ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION is declared below with the Geofencing API it
      serves (§3.4), pinned by DeclaredPermissionsTest.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
      The Wi-Fi suppressor (SPEC.md §6.4, D4): association with the anchor's
      network is what lets the duty cycle do no location work while the user
      is demonstrably still there. State only — Snoozemo never changes Wi-Fi.

      ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE rides in beside it from the `:presence` manifest,
      which declares it for the network callback the SSID read goes through.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_WIFI_STATE" />

    <!--
      Manifest-declared, but granted through a settings screen rather than a
      runtime dialog (SPEC.md §5.2) — there is no runtime prompt for this one,
      which is why onboarding has to send the user to Settings and listen for
      the grant coming back.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NOTIFICATION_POLICY" />

    <!--
      The ongoing "you are snoozed" notification, and the ended one. Requested at
      runtime too (MainActivity): declaring it grants nothing since Android 13,
      and without the grant every degraded-mode and failure message the app
      relies on to not fail silently is dropped by the system.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      A reboot clears every scheduled alarm and restarts nothing, so without this
      an active snooze survives in the platform's zen rule with nothing left in
      the app to end it (SPEC.md §8.1).
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />

    <!--
      Reads the next meeting's end time, so a snooze taken for a meeting can be
      set to end when the meeting does (SPEC.md §4.3). Read on this phone only:
      the time goes onto the ongoing notification's third action and nowhere
      else, no event title or identity is read at all, and nothing about it is
      transmitted or written to the debug log.

      A plain runtime permission with no Play declaration behind it, unlike the
      background location grant, and with no network implication.

      Landed ahead of the Play declarations with the maintainer's decision
      (2026-08-31), overriding `TODO.md`'s note that it should wait for them.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_CALENDAR" />

    <!--
      Firebase Analytics merges `AD_ID` into whatever includes it, and Play's
      Advertising ID declaration is answered "not used"
      (docs/play-store-declarations.md). Removing it keeps that answer true:
      Analytics then reports against the per-install app-instance ID, which is
      specific to this app, cannot be joined to activity elsewhere, and resets
      on a clear-data or reinstall.

      What this costs is the ads-adjacent surface — audience export to Google
      Ads, and the demographics that are inferred from the advertising ID —
      none of which Snoozemo has any use for. `DeclaredPermissionsTest` was
      written to fail the moment Analytics arrived, precisely so this was
      decided rather than inherited; this is that decision.
    -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="com.google.android.gms.permission.AD_ID"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      The Geofencing API needs the background grant to deliver an exit while
      the app holds no foreground service. Declared with the maintainer's approval (2026-08-22); the Play
      Console declaration it commits us to is SPEC.md §3.5's risk.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION" />

    <!--
      The snooze service runs in the foreground while something is actually
      watching for a departure (SPEC.md §6.10). Not for the sake of the
      notification — the ongoing card is posted either way — but for the
      process: an ordinary started service is destroyed routinely, and a device
      log showed the presence watch closing 67 s after arming with nothing
      looking for the next hour, then the geofence exit arriving to a *refused*
      background service start. A foreground service is what keeps the
      collection alive to receive what the fence already delivers.

      The `location` type, chosen by the maintainer (2026-09-08) with the
      policy text in front of them: Android's own page for this type carries an
      "Alternatives" line reading "if your app needs to be triggered when the
      user reaches specific locations, consider using the geofence API
      instead", which is SPEC.md §3's April 2026 concern still live in Google's
      current wording. Snoozemo does both — the fence delivers the exit, the
      service is what survives to hear it — and this is the type that describes
      what the process is doing while it waits.

      The gate in the service is the tracking mode: a duration-only snooze
      starts no watch, so its foreground run does not carry this type.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
      The `specialUse` type, held by every running snooze so the ringer can be
      handed back when it ends (SPEC.md §5.9 rule 5). Chosen by the maintainer
      (2026-10-12): Android 17 silently ignores `setRingerMode` from an app with
      neither a visible activity nor a non-SHORT_SERVICE foreground service, so
      a snooze ending on its own in the background left the phone on vibrate.
      No other type describes it, and `shortService` is the one the platform
      names as not enough. Play needs a declaration for it; the subtype below is
      what that declaration says.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />

    <!--
      Crash reporting and Firebase Analytics use this, and nothing else
      (SPEC.md §12). Both sit behind one consent the user has to give first,
      so the permission is held by a build that sends nothing until asked.

      It was absent until Crashlytics landed, and the day it
      landed the Play Data Safety answer moved off "no data collected, no data
      shared" onto crash logs, diagnostics and the installation identifier
      Crashlytics records; Analytics later added app interactions and its own
      app-instance identifier — docs/play-store-declarations.md carries the
      answers and docs/PRIVACY.md the user-facing statement.

      The floor of SPEC.md §12 is unchanged by this permission — no
      coordinates, no SSID or BSSID, no user-typed place name goes anywhere,
      and the on-device debug log still leaves the phone only when the user
      shares it. What Crashlytics sends is a stack trace; what Analytics sends
      is the events the SDK collects on its own. The user can turn both off in
      Settings, with one switch, and neither sends anything until they say yes.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

    <application
        android:name=".SnoozemoApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Snoozemo">

        <!--
          WorkManager's automatic App Startup initializer is removed, and only
          its own: initializing on process startup puts database work ahead of
          a cold tile tap's ACTION_ARM (SPEC.md §4.1; Codex, PR #75), and the
          first thing that needs WorkManager is the backstop schedule, which
          runs after the rule is already on. The Application provides the
          configuration, so WorkManager initializes on first use instead. The
          provider itself is merged, not removed — other libraries' startup
          entries keep working.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.startup.InitializationProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.androidx-startup"
            android:exported="false"
            tools:node="merge">
            <meta-data
                android:name="androidx.work.WorkManagerInitializer"
                android:value="androidx.startup"
                tools:node="remove" />
        </provider>

        <activity
            android:name=".ui.MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
            <!--
              Long-pressing the tile lands here (SPEC.md §4.2). It is also the
              rule's configurationActivity, so the entry Settings offers next to
              Snoozemo's DND rule goes to the same place.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.quicksettings.action.QS_TILE_PREFERENCES" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          Started by the tile's trampoline; owns the running snooze. Typed
          `specialUse` for the whole snooze, so the ringer can be handed back at
          its end, and `location` while a presence watch needs keeping alive
          (see the two typed grants above).
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".snooze.SnoozeService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="location|specialUse">
            <property
                android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
                android:value="Restores the ringer mode when a Do Not Disturb snooze ends" />
        </service>

        <!--
          Transparent, and on the arm path: it starts the service before any UI
          so arming never waits on rendering (SPEC.md §6.9). The action is how
          :tile launches it without a compile-time dependency on :app.

          Deliberately not android:noHistory: this activity outlives its own
          first frame when it hosts the notification-permission dialog, and will
          again for the Phase 4 end-condition sheet. noHistory would finish it
          the moment the dialog took the foreground, dropping the result. It
          always finishes itself instead, and excludeFromRecents keeps it out of
          the recents list either way.

          exported="false", and no intent-filter: an exported trampoline lets any
          app on the phone silence it for the default cap. :tile names this class
          explicitly instead, and the PendingIntent the system invokes carries
          this app's identity, so nothing here needs to be reachable from
          outside.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".snooze.TileTrampolineActivity"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleInstance"
            android:showWhenLocked="true"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Snoozemo.Transparent" />

        <receiver
            android:name=".snooze.CapAlarmReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!--
          Backs the `Dismiss` action on the stuck-rule notification. Not
          exported: the only sender is our own PendingIntent, and letting anyone
          else clear that card would remove the one affordance pointing at a
          rule that may still be silencing the phone.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".snooze.NotificationActionReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />

        <!-- Exported because the sender is the system (SPEC.md §8.1). -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".snooze.BootReceiver"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
                <!--
                  An update leaves the same wreckage a reboot does — a record on
                  disk, no process alive, and the zen rule still on — so it takes
                  the same repair. It is also the one moment a snooze outlives
                  the build that armed it, which is when a record written by an
                  older build meets a newer one that will not vouch for it: left
                  unhandled, that reads as no snooze at all while the phone stays
                  quiet (SPEC.md §12).
                -->
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          The cap alarm counts in elapsed realtime, which a wall-clock change
          does not move — so a clock moved *forward* past the deadline leaves
          the countdown reading zero while the alarm waits out the original
          duration (SPEC.md §7). This is what closes that gap. TIME_SET is one of
          the implicit broadcasts still delivered to manifest receivers,
          precisely so apps can reschedule alarms after a clock change.

          Not exported: the system reaches a private receiver for its own
          broadcasts, and exporting would let any app forge a clock change and
          drive a cap check — ending someone's snooze on demand. Matches the
          sibling Simmo repo's DataWatchReceiver, which takes the same
          system-broadcast-only receiver private for the same reason.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".snooze.TimeChangedReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.TIME_SET" />
                <!--
                  A timezone change moves no deadline — the alarms count in
                  elapsed realtime — but it does move how the ongoing card's
                  `Until <time>` action reads (SPEC.md §4.3), so the receiver
                  reposts. Also an implicit broadcast still delivered to
                  manifest receivers, and private for the same reason TIME_SET
                  is.
                -->
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.TIMEZONE_CHANGED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          Off until the app says otherwise. Crashlytics would otherwise start
          collecting during process creation — before Snoozemo has read the
          stored opt-out — so an install where the user has turned reporting
          off would still upload the first crash after every launch. The gate
          in CrashReporter applies the stored choice at startup; both SDK
          switches persist what they are given, so later launches honor the
          last-known choice even before the store has been read.

          The cost of this ordering is the narrow window it leaves: a crash
          between process creation and the gate applying `true` is not
          reported. That is the right way round — an unreported crash is a
          missing diagnostic, a wrongly-reported one breaks a promise made in
          Settings.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="firebase_crashlytics_collection_enabled"
            android:value="false" />
        <!--
            Analytics needs its own switch, and the reasoning above is why
            rather than a second copy of it: Firebase initializes every SDK
            from the same ContentProvider, so an SDK without a default here
            decides for itself before any of Snoozemo's code has run. Analytics
            is the one where that bites hardest — it records its own automatic
            events with no call site of ours to gate, so an install that has
            not answered the question yet would already have sent `first_open`
            and `session_start` by the time it is asked.

            The advertising ID is switched off separately below; the permission
            itself is removed in the main manifest, so the identifier that
            remains is per-install and cannot be joined to activity in other
            apps.
        -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="firebase_analytics_collection_enabled"
            android:value="false" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="google_analytics_adid_collection_enabled"
            android:value="false" />
    </application>

</manifest>
